# 0017. Version the canonical JSON, and make readers reject a version they don't read

**Status:** Accepted **Date:** 2026-10-24

## Context

The canonical JSON has changed shape under its readers once already: ADR 0009 replaced inline props with ids, and nothing told a reader of the old shape. Three packages parse it (`caliper-docgen-napi`'s types, the Vite plugin, the Storybook addon), and so will other tools, such as an agent server linking `caliper-docgen-core`. Semver on the packages and changesets does not guard the JSON: a reader and the binding can be installed at different releases, and the mismatch surfaces as `undefined is not iterable` somewhere downstream.

## Decision

Every canonical JSON document carries `schemaVersion`, an integer: `ExtractionOutput` (CLI `--json` and `--format canonical`, NAPI `extractAll`, `initializeSession`), `IncrementalUpdate`, and the session index (`initializeSessionIndex`). `SCHEMA_VERSION` in `crates/core/src/types/output.rs` is the one definition. The shape ADR 0009 describes is version 1.

- **Bump** when a reader of the previous version would misread or lose data: a field removed, renamed or retyped, a field's meaning changed, or references and tables restructured.
- **Don't bump** for additions a reader can ignore: a new field, a new `detectedBy` value, a new diagnostic code. Readers must tolerate those.
- **Readers** accept their own version and reject any other with an error naming both versions. Output with no `schemaVersion` predates it and has version 1's shape.
- **Release:** a bump goes out in a changeset marked breaking for the core crate and every package that reads the JSON, so writer and readers move together.
- `--format rdt`, `--format storybook` and `--format toon` have other tools' shapes and carry no version. The parse cache has its own `CACHE_SCHEMA_VERSION`.

## Consequences

- A mismatched install fails at the first read with a message that says what to update, instead of a type error deep in a consumer.
- Every shape change needs a deliberate bump, a changeset and a reader update. That is the point, and it costs a small coordinated release each time.
- An old reader cannot read new output at all, even the parts that didn't change. We accept that over a reader that half-works.
- `ExtractionOutput`'s `Deserialize` rejects a version other than `SCHEMA_VERSION`. Reading older versions would need a migration per version, which nothing needs today.

## Alternatives considered

- **Semver of the package as the contract:** readers and the binding are separate installs, so the package version does not say which shape a document has.
- **A range or a minor version in the document:** none of the three readers can use a "compatible up to" statement, and the "don't bump" rule already covers compatible additions.
# 0009. Write each distinct prop once in the canonical output

**Status:** Accepted **Date:** 2026-10-21

## Context

Inherited props repeat on every component that inherits them. On Mantine, 19,506 props hold 3,137 distinct values, and the canonical JSON is 19.3 MB. The Vite plugin serves that whole output as its virtual module and re-serves it after every edit, so the browser downloads and parses all of it (34 ms in `JSON.parse` on Mantine) each time. Storybook does not read this format: both its build-time `__docgenInfo` and its Storybook 11 docgen providers take RDT-shaped props, which an integration converts to.

## Decision

The canonical JSON (CLI `--json` and `--format canonical`, NAPI `extractAll` and `initializeSession`, watch updates) writes each distinct prop once, in a top-level `props` table, and components refer to it by id.

- `components[key].props` and `components[key].notableInherited` map a prop name to an id; `props[id]` is the full prop.
- An id is the 64-bit FNV-1a hash of the prop's compact JSON, as 16 hex digits. The same prop gets the same id in every output and update a build writes, so a client can merge tables. Two different props with one id fail serialization instead of one silently replacing the other.
- An `IncrementalUpdate` carries `props` with every entry its `updatedComponents` reference. A client merges it into its table and may drop ids no component references any more.
- Interning is a wire-format concern only. The Rust types keep each `ComponentEntry`'s props inline, and `ComponentEntry` on its own still serializes them inline.
- `--format rdt` and `--format storybook` stay inline: they exist to match other tools' shapes.

## Consequences

- Mantine's output drops from 19.3 MB to 3.6 MB and its parse from 34 ms to 4.5 ms; Spectrum's from 6.9 MB to 4.2 MB.
- Breaking for every reader of the canonical format: a prop is now one lookup away. `packages/napi/index.d.ts` types the table, and readers that want inline props expand them.
- Ids are opaque. They change when a prop's serialized form changes, including across versions, so nothing may store one beyond the output it came from.
- A hash collision fails the whole write. At 100,000 distinct props the chance is about 3 in 10 billion.

## Alternatives considered

- **Positional ids** (index into a table array): shorter, but the same prop moves between revisions, so every watch update would have to resend the table.
- **Interning only types**: the type is half of a repeated prop; descriptions, parents and tags repeat too. Mantine stays at 10.4 MB.
- **Compression only**: gzip already shrinks the output to 0.83 MB in transit, but the reader still parses 19.3 MB.
    pub removed_diagnostics: Vec<Diagnostic>,
    pub duration_ms: u64,
}

impl Serialize for IncrementalUpdate {
    fn serialize<S: serde::Serializer>(&self, serializer: S) -> Result<S::Ok, S::Error> {
        use crate::types::output::interned::{intern, InternedComponent, Tables};
        use serde::ser::SerializeStruct;

        let IncrementalUpdate {
            revision,
            updated_components,
            updated_keys,
            removed_keys,
            affected_files,
            diagnostics,
            removed_diagnostics,
            duration_ms,
        } = self;
        let entries: Vec<&ComponentEntry> = updated_components.iter().collect();
        let (ids, mut tables) = intern::<S::Error>(&entries)?;
        let added = tables.intern_affects::<S::Error>(diagnostics)?;
        let removed = tables.intern_affects::<S::Error>(removed_diagnostics)?;
        let components: Vec<InternedComponent<'_>> =
            entries.iter().zip(&ids).map(|(entry, ids)| InternedComponent { entry, ids }).collect();
        let mut out = serializer.serialize_struct("IncrementalUpdate", 9 + Tables::FIELDS)?;
        out.serialize_field("schemaVersion", &crate::types::SCHEMA_VERSION)?;
        out.serialize_field("revision", revision)?;
        out.serialize_field("updatedComponents", &components)?;
        tables.write(&mut out)?;
        out.serialize_field("updatedKeys", updated_keys)?;
        out.serialize_field("removedKeys", removed_keys)?;
        out.serialize_field("affectedFiles", affected_files)?;
        out.serialize_field("diagnostics", &added)?;
        out.serialize_field("removedDiagnostics", &removed)?;
        out.serialize_field("durationMs", duration_ms)?;
        out.end()
    }
}

/// A session's index: where each component lives and the run's diagnostics, without props. A client that routes by file
/// and name reads this instead of the whole output.
struct SessionIndex<'a> {
    locations: &'a [ComponentLocation],
    output: &'a ExtractionOutput,
}

impl Serialize for SessionIndex<'_> {
    fn serialize<S: serde::Serializer>(&self, serializer: S) -> Result<S::Ok, S::Error> {
        use crate::types::output::interned::{InternedDiagnostics, NameLists};
        use serde::ser::SerializeStruct;

        #[derive(Serialize)]
        #[serde(rename_all = "camelCase")]
        struct Location<'a> {
            file_path: &'a str,
            display_name: &'a str,
        }

        let mut lists = NameLists::new();
        let diagnostics = InternedDiagnostics::new::<S::Error>(&mut lists, &self.output.diagnostics)?;
        let locations: std::collections::BTreeMap<&str, Location<'_>> = self
            .locations
            .iter()
            .map(|at| (at.key.as_str(), Location { file_path: at.file_path.as_str(), display_name: &at.display_name }))
            .collect();
        let mut out = serializer.serialize_struct("SessionIndex", 4)?;
        out.serialize_field("schemaVersion", &crate::types::SCHEMA_VERSION)?;
        out.serialize_field("components", &locations)?;
        out.serialize_field("nameLists", &lists)?;
        out.serialize_field("diagnostics", &diagnostics)?;
        out.end()
    }
}

/// Serialize a session's index to a JSON string: `schemaVersion`, where each component lives (`components`), and the
/// run's `diagnostics` with the `nameLists` they refer to. The props stay in the session. `locations` is
/// [`WatchSession::component_index`]: a lazy session's output has no components, so the index cannot come from it.
pub fn session_index_to_json(
    locations: &[ComponentLocation],
    output: &ExtractionOutput,
) -> Result<String, serde_json::Error> {
    serde_json::to_string(&SessionIndex { locations, output })
}

